134-<details>
135-<summary>Differences from Rails Campfire</summary>
136-
137-- **Bots:** bot ownership and codes use an additive `chat_bots` table linked to ordinary users.
138-  The Rust bot UI/API is not available when switching back to the older Rails image; existing
139-  users, memberships, and messages remain compatible. Bot API bodies are literal UTF-8 text,
140-  not HTML or form fields. A bot must belong to the destination room. Optional webhook URLs use
141-  an additive `chat_bot_webhooks` table. Mention callbacks are best-effort background jobs (no
142-  retries or persistence across crashes), allow internal HTTP(S) services, and never follow
143-  redirects. Bots join open rooms only when "Add to all open rooms" is enabled; people still
144-  join automatically. Open-room forms allow selecting individual bots. Turning the bot switch
145-  off preserves memberships. A first non-direct room membership
146-  sends a `joined` callback, whose text/HTML reply is handled like a mention reply. Additive
147-  `chat_bot_auto_join` and `chat_bot_room_joins` tables store opt-ins and deduplicate greetings.
148-  Bot-authored messages do not trigger callbacks. See [bot webhooks](docs/bot-webhooks.md).
149-  Administrators can set **Bots per member** in account settings (default 5, zero allowed).
150-  Only active bots count; administrators are exempt. Lowering the limit preserves existing
151-  bots. This Rust-only limit is stored as `bots_per_member` in the account settings JSON.
